description:
Write the logic at the heart of a fast file copier built on overlapped I/O and a completion port (lesson 7.03). The engine decides which reads and writes to start; the real program would start them with `ReadFile` and `WriteFile` and feed the completions from `GetQueuedCompletionStatus` back into the engine. The tests play the part of Windows: they carry out the operations and complete them **in a scrambled order**, with errors and cancellation.

```cpp
enum class OpKind { Read, Write };
struct Op { OpKind kind; std::uint32_t id; std::uint64_t offset; std::uint32_t length; std::string data; };
struct Completion { std::uint32_t id; std::uint32_t error; std::string data; };

constexpr std::uint32_t ERROR_HANDLE_EOF = 38, ERROR_OPERATION_ABORTED = 995;

class CopyEngine
{
public:
    CopyEngine(std::uint64_t fileSize, std::uint32_t chunkSize, int maxOutstanding);
    std::vector<Op> start();
    std::vector<Op> onCompletion(const Completion& c);
    std::vector<std::uint32_t> cancel();
    bool finished() const;
    bool failed() const;
    bool cancelled() const;
    std::uint32_t error() const;
    std::uint64_t bytesWritten() const;
    int outstanding() const;
};
```

**Chunks.** The file is copied in chunks of `chunkSize` bytes at offsets 0, `chunkSize`, `2 × chunkSize` ...; the last chunk is shorter if the size is not a multiple. An empty file needs no operations and is finished at once.

**Operations.** Every operation the engine asks for gets the next id, starting at 1. A read asks for `length` bytes at `offset` (its `data` is empty); a write gives the `data` to write at `offset` (its `length` is the data's size). At most `maxOutstanding` operations, reads and writes together, are ever in flight: started and not yet completed.

- **start** returns the first reads, in offset order, as many as the limit allows.
- **onCompletion** receives the completion of an operation in flight (an unknown id throws `std::invalid_argument`), removes it from the in-flight set, and returns the operations to start next:
  - A successful **read** (`error` 0) must carry exactly the bytes asked for; then the engine returns a **write** of those bytes at the same offset. If it carries fewer or more (the file changed under us), the copy **fails** with `ERROR_HANDLE_EOF`.
  - A successful **write** adds its bytes to `bytesWritten`, and the engine starts the **next read**, if any chunks are left and the limit allows.
  - A completion with an **error** makes the copy **fail** with that error (the first error is kept), unless the copy was cancelled and the error is `ERROR_OPERATION_ABORTED`.
  - Once failed or cancelled, the engine starts nothing new; it only takes in the remaining completions.
- **cancel** stops the copy and returns the ids of every operation in flight, in ascending order, for the program to pass to `CancelIoEx`.
- **finished** is true when every byte has been written, nothing is in flight, and the copy neither failed nor was cancelled.

